{"version":1,"readings":[{"id":"rd1","title":"The Testing Effect","level":"B2","minutes":4,"text":"Imagine two students. One rereads a chapter until it feels familiar. The other closes the book and tries to recall everything she can. A week later, the second student remembers far more. Psychologists call this the testing effect, and it has survived a century of attempts to prove it wrong. The reason is effort. When your brain has to reconstruct a memory, the connections behind it grow stronger. Rereading, by contrast, only makes the text feel comfortable, and comfort is a poor signal of learning. Teachers who quiz students at the start of class, with no grades attached, often see exam scores rise. The quiz is not measurement. It is the lesson itself. If you want a language to stick, do not reread your notes: close them, and pull the words out of your head.","glossary":[{"w":"recall","fa":"به یاد آوردن"},{"w":"reconstruct","fa":"بازسازی کردن"},{"w":"by contrast","fa":"در مقابل"},{"w":"signal","fa":"نشانه"},{"w":"attached","fa":"پیوسته، وابسته"},{"w":"stick","fa":"در ذهن ماندن"}],"questions":[{"q":"What does the text say about rereading?","choices":["It builds strong memories","It creates a feeling of comfort that can hide weak learning","It works only for languages","It is the fastest way to memorize"],"a":1},{"q":"Why do ungraded quizzes help?","choices":["They reduce exam stress","They replace homework","The act of retrieval strengthens memory","They give teachers more data"],"a":2},{"q":"The word it in 'it has survived' refers to","choices":["a century","the testing effect","a chapter","a student"],"a":1},{"q":"What advice does the writer give?","choices":["Study with notes open","Test yourself before rereading","Avoid comfort entirely","Reread twice, then test"],"a":1}]},{"id":"rd2","title":"An Email About a Delay","level":"B2","minutes":3,"text":"Subject: Delivery update for order 8421. Dear Ms. Rahimi, I am writing to let you know that the sensor modules for your order are running three days behind schedule. The supplier traced the delay to a shortage of a single component, and rather than ship a partial batch, we chose to hold everything until the full set clears quality control. I realize this is inconvenient, and I take full responsibility for not flagging the risk earlier. As a gesture, we will cover the express shipping on your next order. The revised delivery date is Thursday the 14th, and I will personally confirm the dispatch on Wednesday. If the new date causes problems on your side, please call me directly and we will work out an alternative. Kind regards, D. Karimi, Logistics Lead.","glossary":[{"w":"running behind schedule","fa":"عقب‌تر از برنامه بودن"},{"w":"shortage","fa":"کمبود"},{"w":"batch","fa":"بچه، گروه محموله"},{"w":"flagging","fa":"گوشزد کردن، علامت دادن"},{"w":"as a gesture","fa":"به‌عنوان ابراز حسن نیت"},{"w":"dispatch","fa":"ارسال محموله"}],"questions":[{"q":"Why was the whole order held back?","choices":["The factory closed","To ship it complete rather than in parts","The customer changed the address","Express shipping was unavailable"],"a":1},{"q":"What does the writer offer?","choices":["A refund","Free products","Paid shipping on the next order","A discount code"],"a":2},{"q":"'I take full responsibility' is an example of","choices":["blaming the supplier","accepting accountability","making an excuse","offering a discount"],"a":1},{"q":"When will dispatch be confirmed?","choices":["Thursday the 14th","Wednesday","Immediately","Next month"],"a":1}]},{"id":"rd3","title":"Habits and the Brain","level":"B2","minutes":4,"text":"Habits are the brain's way of saving effort. When a behavior repeats in the same setting, control over it slowly shifts from conscious decision to automatic routine, which frees attention for harder problems. This shift happens in a region deep below the cortex, and it explains why you can drive home while planning dinner. The catch is that the mechanism cannot tell a useful habit from a harmful one; it simply automates whatever repeats. That is why the environment matters more than willpower. People who want to read more rarely succeed by trying harder at bedtime. They succeed when a book sits on the pillow and the phone charges in another room. Design the setting first, and the behavior tends to follow. Breaking a habit, meanwhile, rarely means deleting it. The old loop remains, ready to fire, and the practical goal is to build a stronger loop that fires sooner.","glossary":[{"w":"conscious","fa":"هوشیارانه، خودآگاه"},{"w":"cortex","fa":"قشر مغز"},{"w":"willpower","fa":"اراده"},{"w":"tend to","fa":"تمایل داشتن به"},{"w":"loop","fa":"چرخه"},{"w":"fire","fa":"فعال شدن"}],"questions":[{"q":"Why does the brain build habits?","choices":["To make behavior harder","To save conscious effort","To store memories","To reward repetition"],"a":1},{"q":"What matters more than willpower, according to the text?","choices":["Motivation quotes","The physical environment","Sleep quality","Peer pressure"],"a":1},{"q":"'The catch' introduces","choices":["a benefit","a drawback","an example","a question"],"a":1},{"q":"Breaking a habit, the text argues, means","choices":["erasing the old loop","weakening a stronger new loop into place","avoiding all routines","relying on willpower"],"a":1}]},{"id":"rd4","title":"The Quiet Engineering of Cities","level":"C1","minutes":5,"text":"The most consequential infrastructure in a city is the kind residents never see. Beneath the asphalt runs a nervous system of mains, sensors and control loops that decides, minute by minute, how water pressure distributes itself across a hillside district. For most of the twentieth century this system was engineered for redundancy: any single failure had to leave the network functional, and the price was chronic inefficiency. The current generation of utility managers argues that telemetry changes the calculation. Continuous pressure data, fed into predictive models, allows operators to intervene before a pipe bursts rather than after, and to throttle pumps in response to demand that was previously invisible. Critics counter that instrumentation without institutional competence merely documents failures more precisely. The evidence so far is partial, but the direction is clear: the value has shifted from building robust systems to operating adaptive ones, and the cities that internalize this distinction will spend conspicuously less on emergency repairs than those that do not.","glossary":[{"w":"consequential","fa":"پرمایه، مهم و اثرگذار"},{"w":"redundancy","fa":"افزونگی برای اطمینان"},{"w":"chronic","fa":"مزمن"},{"w":"telemetry","fa":"سنجش از راه دور"},{"w":"throttle","fa":"تنظیم و کاهش دادن"},{"w":"internalize","fa":"درونی کردن، درون‌سازی کردن"},{"w":"conspicuously","fa":"به‌شکل چشمگیر"}],"questions":[{"q":"What trade-off did twentieth century designs accept?","choices":["Lower pressure for lower cost","Inefficiency in exchange for resilience","Manual control for accuracy","Cheaper pipes for shorter lives"],"a":1},{"q":"What do critics argue about telemetry?","choices":["It is too expensive","It documents failure better than it prevents it","It invades privacy","It requires replacing all pipes"],"a":1},{"q":"'The direction is clear' suggests the author believes","choices":["the evidence is complete","the trend favors adaptive operation","critics will be proven right","cities will abandon telemetry"],"a":1},{"q":"The phrase 'nervous system' is an example of","choices":["hyperbole","metaphor","irony","euphemism"],"a":1}]},{"id":"rd5","title":"Why Bilinguals Hesitate","level":"C1","minutes":5,"text":"For decades, bilingual speakers were said to enjoy amental workout from constantly inhibiting one of their languages. Recent repainting of the evidence has softened that claim considerably, yet something genuinely interesting remains in the hesitation data. When a bilingual names an object, both languages offer candidates, and the competition between them is measurable in milliseconds. What interests researchers is not the delay itself but its distribution: hesitation clusters at precisely the points where the two languages diverge in structure, not merely in vocabulary. This supports a view of bilingual memory as deeply intertwined rather than stored in parallel warehouses. It also suggests why immersion matters. The brain calibrates the competition to context; learners who consistently operate in one setting develop a faster suppression of the irrelevant lexicon. The pedagogical implication is uncomfortable for curricula built on isolated word lists: language lives in situations, and the situation is what the memory actually encodes.","glossary":[{"w":"inhibiting","fa":"مهار کردن"},{"w":"repainting of the evidence","fa":"بازخوانی شواهد (استعاره)"},{"w":"diverge","fa":"واگرا شدن"},{"w":"intertwined","fa":"در هم تنیده"},{"w":"calibrates","fa":"کالیبره می‌کند، تنظیم می‌کند"},{"w":"lexicon","fa":"واژگان"},{"w":"encodes","fa":"کدگذاری می‌کند"}],"questions":[{"q":"What has happened to the 'bilingual advantage' claim?","choices":["It has been proven fully","It has been softened by re-examined evidence","It applies only to children","It was never studied"],"a":1},{"q":"Where does hesitation cluster?","choices":["At random points","Where languages differ in structure","At the start of sentences","Only among novice learners"],"a":1},{"q":"The 'parallel warehouses' phrase describes a view the author","choices":["endorses","argues against","quotes approvingly","proved experimentally"],"a":1},{"q":"What pedagogical implication does the author draw?","choices":["Word lists should be longer","Context drives what memory encodes","Immersion is unnecessary","Hesitation should be trained away"],"a":1}]},{"id":"rd6","title":"The Economics of Attention","level":"C1","minutes":5,"text":"Every free product on the internet runs on the same quiet transaction: the user pays with attention, and attention is resold to advertisers. What makes this economy unusual is not the exchange itself, which the broadcasting industry perfected decades ago, but the precision with which attention can now be measured, auctioned and packaged. An impression once purchased the possibility of a glance; the modern equivalent buys a probabilistic claim on a second of gaze, priced in milliseconds. Defenders of the model point out that it funds an astonishing quantity of services that users would otherwise ration. Skeptics reply that the externalities are no smaller than those of any extractive industry: fractured focus, engineered outrage, and a public sphere calibrated for arousal rather than deliberation. Regulation is arriving, unevenly. The deeper question is whether attention, once financialized, can be de-financialized at the level of individual habit, or whether users will need structural help to reclaim the hours they already lent away.","glossary":[{"w":"resold","fa":"بازفروشی شده"},{"w":"auctioned","fa":"حراج شده"},{"w":"probabilistic","fa":"احتمالاتی"},{"w":"externalities","fa":"پیامدهای بیرونی"},{"w":"extractive","fa":"استخراجی، خلاص‌کننده"},{"w":"deliberation","fa":"سنجیدگی، تعقل"},{"w":"financialized","fa":"مالی‌سازی شده"}],"questions":[{"q":"What is genuinely new in this economy?","choices":["Selling attention to advertisers","The precision of measuring and pricing attention","Free products","Advertising itself"],"a":1},{"q":"Skeptics compare attention extraction to","choices":["broadcasting","an extractive industry","gambling","banking"],"a":1},{"q":"The final question concerns whether","choices":["regulation will arrive","users can reclaim attention through habit alone","advertisers will leave","platforms will charge fees"],"a":1},{"q":"The tone of the passage is best described as","choices":["openly hostile","measured and analytical","promotional","satirical"],"a":1}]}]}