answered every question with remarkable ___.","choices":["fluently","fluent","fluidity","fluency"],"a":"fluency","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"v6","sec":"vocab","rung":"C1","q":"The two accounts of the accident are impossible to ___.","choices":["collide","reconcile","reconciliate","coordinate"],"a":"reconcile","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"v7","sec":"vocab","rung":"C1","q":"His calm tone was a deliberate ___ to keep things short.","choices":["device","devise","devising","devised"],"a":"device","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c1","sec":"collocation","rung":"B1","q":"I need to ___ a decision soon.","choices":["do","make","take","have"],"a":"make","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c2","sec":"collocation","rung":"B2","q":"We must ___ these risks into account.","choices":["make","take","bring","hold"],"a":"take","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c3","sec":"collocation","rung":"B2","q":"The director ___ pressure on the team to ship early.","choices":["made","gave","put","took"],"a":"put","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c4","sec":"collocation","rung":"B2+","q":"Her argument doesn't ___ water.","choices":["carry","keep","fill","hold"],"a":"hold","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c5","sec":"collocation","rung":"B2+","q":"The two sides finally ___ an agreement.","choices":["struck","hit","beat","built"],"a":"struck","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c6","sec":"collocation","rung":"C1","q":"The speech was carefully ___ to avoid controversy.","choices":["said","worded","spoken","told"],"a":"worded","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"c7","sec":"collocation","rung":"C1","q":"He ___ short of calling the report a lie.","choices":["stopped","paused","avoided","refused"],"a":"stopped","trap":"word_choice"},{"id":"r1","sec":"reading","rung":"B2","q":"Most learners review new words on the same day they meet them, then wonder why the words vanish by Friday. Studies of memory keep pointing the same way: recall that happens after a night of sleep, when the trace has partly faded, leaves a far stronger mark than recall that happens while the page is still open.\n\nWhy does spaced review beat same-day review?","choices":["Because the memory trace has partly faded, so recall has to work harder","Because words studied at night are easier to read the next day","Because sleep makes the material feel more familiar on the page","Because reviewing later takes less time than reviewing immediately"],"a":"Because the memory trace has partly faded, so recall has to work harder","trap":"fluency_illusion"},{"id":"r2","sec":"reading","rung":"B2+","q":"Psychologist Robert Bjork argues that some obstacles during practice are 'desirable difficulties': they slow down visible progress while quietly strengthening what stays in memory. A practice session that feels smooth can therefore be producing less learning than a session that feels like a struggle.\n\nWhat does the phrase 'desirable difficulties' suggest?","choices":["Teachers should remove obstacles so practice feels smooth","Difficult materials are always more effective than easy ones","Struggle during practice can strengthen long-term retention","Progress that feels slow is a sign of poor practice methods"],"a":"Struggle during practice can strengthen long-term retention","trap":"fluency_illusion"},{"id":"r3","sec":"reading","rung":"C1","q":"Readers often mistake the ease of rereading a chapter for evidence that they know it. The pages feel familiar, the argument arrives without effort, and a test the next morning reveals how little survived. Familiarity, researchers warn, is a poor proxy for knowledge.\n\nThe passage implies that learners who feel fluent while rereading ___.","choices":["have stored the chapter durably in long-term memory","often mistake familiarity for genuine knowledge","should switch to harder chapters immediately after","benefit most from reviewing the same chapter again"],"a":"often mistake familiarity for genuine knowledge","trap":"fluency_illusion"},{"id":"r4","sec":"reading","rung":"C1","q":"A study team compared three revision schedules across a term. Learners who reviewed a topic the day it was taught rated their confidence highest, yet scored lowest on the delayed final. The group whose reviews began only when material started to slip performed best, despite reporting the most effort during practice.\n\nWhich sentence best captures the study's conclusion?","choices":["Review works best while the material still feels fresh","Confidence during revision predicts final performance","Effortful practice is a symptom of a poor study schedule","Review pays off most when it starts at the edge of forgetting"],"a":"Review pays off most when it starts at the edge of forgetting","trap":"fluency_illusion"}]}